 Values must match the normalized runtime shape, not the raw incoming payload.
 
+## Wildcards
+
+Every field above except `mentioned` accepts `*`, which matches any sequence of
+characters including an empty one. A value with no `*` is compared exactly, so
+existing rules keep behaving as before.
+
+This is what lets one rule cover a family of chats instead of naming each one —
+useful when chat IDs are created dynamically and a per-chat rule list would grow
+without bound:
+
+```json
+{
+  "name": "project-seedtrial",
+  "agent": "seedtrial",
+  "when": {
+    "channel": "pico",
+    "chat": "direct:pico:p.seedtrial.*"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+`*` is the only special character. `?`, `[`, `]` and `\` are matched literally,
+so a pattern can never be malformed — a rule either matches what you wrote or it
+does not.
+
+Two things to watch:
+
+- A wildcard rule can overlap a narrower one. Rules are evaluated top to bottom,
+  so keep the specific rule above the wildcard (see Rule Ordering below).
+- `"*"` on its own matches everything for that field. As the only constraint in a
+  rule, it makes that rule a catch-all that no later rule can ever be reached
+  past.
+
 ## Rule Ordering
 
 Put more specific rules before broader rules.
